
// ============================================================
// TEST DATA — q: question, o: [options a-d], a: correct index,
// r: rationale, ref: Student Guide reference (chapter, EO).
// Source: NIFE Aerodynamics Student Guide
// (NAVAVSCOLSCOM-SG-200, Module/Unit 2, 01AUG2020).
// Ch 1 = G0201 Basic Theory and Lift Production, Ch 2 = G0202
// Drag and Stalls, Ch 3 = G0203 Performance Characteristics,
// Ch 4 = G0204 Maneuvering and Hazards.
// ============================================================
var TESTS = [
{
  name: "Test 1 — Easy",
  desc: "Single-concept recall drawn directly from Student Guide language.",
  questions: [
    {q:"A vector is a quantity that represents:", o:["Mass and volume","Direction only","Magnitude only","Magnitude and direction"], a:3, r:"A vector represents both magnitude and direction, shown graphically as an arrow whose length is the magnitude and whose heading is the direction. A quantity with magnitude only (like time or volume) is a scalar.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.1"},
    {q:"Air density is defined as:", o:["The amount of water vapor in the air","The average kinetic energy of air particles","The total mass of air particles per unit of volume","The weight of a column of air over a given area"], a:2, r:"Density is mass per unit volume. The weight of a column of air over a given area defines static pressure, water vapor content defines humidity, and average kinetic energy of particles defines temperature.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.2"},
    {q:"A moment is created when:", o:["A mass is attracted toward the center of the earth","A mass is multiplied by its acceleration","A force is applied at some distance from an axis or fulcrum, tending to produce rotation about that point","A body has the capacity to do work"], a:2, r:"A moment equals force times the perpendicular distance (moment arm) from the point of rotation and tends to produce rotation. Attraction toward the earth's center is weight, mass times acceleration is force, and capacity to do work is energy.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.3"},
    {q:"Equilibrium flight exists when:", o:["Thrust equals lift and drag equals weight","The airplane is in straight and level flight only","The sum of all forces and the sum of all moments around the center of gravity equal zero","The sum of all forces equals zero, regardless of the moments"], a:2, r:"Equilibrium is the absence of both linear and angular acceleration: zero net force AND zero net moment about the CG. It is not limited to level flight — a stabilized climb at constant velocity is also equilibrium. Pairing thrust with lift mismatches the opposing forces, and ignoring moments omits half the requirement.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.4"},
    {q:"Air temperature decreases with an increase in altitude at an average lapse rate of ___ until approximately ___.", o:["2 degrees C per 1,000 ft; 18,000 ft","5 degrees C per 1,000 ft; 66,000 ft","3.57 degrees C per 1,000 ft; 36,000 ft","2 degrees C per 1,000 ft; 36,000 ft"], a:3, r:"The average lapse rate is 2 degrees C (3.57 degrees F) per 1,000 feet up to about 36,000 feet. The 3.57 figure is the Fahrenheit rate, not Celsius.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.6"},
    {q:"As humidity increases, air density:", o:["Decreases","First increases, then decreases","Remains constant","Increases"], a:0, r:"Water molecules displace an equal number of air molecules but have less mass, so the same volume holds less total mass — density decreases as humidity increases.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.7"},
    {q:"The General Gas Law sets the relationship between which three properties of air?", o:["Pressure, density, and temperature","Pressure, volume, and humidity","Density, viscosity, and temperature","Pressure, temperature, and velocity"], a:0, r:"P = rho R T relates pressure, density, and temperature (R is a constant for a given gas). Humidity, viscosity, and velocity are not terms in the General Gas Law.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.8"},
    {q:"According to the continuity equation, if the cross-sectional area of a streamtube decreases, the velocity of the airflow:", o:["Decreases","Remains constant","Increases","Drops to zero"], a:2, r:"A1V1 = A2V2: area and velocity at any point in a streamtube are inversely related, so a smaller area forces a higher velocity to keep both sides equal.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.10"},
    {q:"According to Bernoulli's equation, total pressure is the sum of:", o:["Static pressure and dynamic pressure","Static pressure and ambient pressure","Atmospheric pressure and vapor pressure","Dynamic pressure and ram pressure"], a:0, r:"PT = PS + q. Static pressure is the potential energy of the air and dynamic pressure is its kinetic energy; in a closed system their sum (total pressure) is constant. Ambient, ram, and vapor pressure are not the Bernoulli terms.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.11"},
    {q:"Climbing from sea level to 10,000 feet at a constant indicated airspeed, true airspeed will be ___ indicated airspeed.", o:["Equal to","Less than","Impossible to compare to","Greater than"], a:3, r:"The pitot-static system is calibrated for standard sea level density. As density decreases with altitude, holding IAS constant means the airplane must actually move faster through the air mass, so TAS increases above IAS. They are equal only at sea level on a standard day.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.12"},
    {q:"The chordline of an airfoil is:", o:["An infinitely long, straight line passing through the leading and trailing edges","The length of the wing measured from wingtip to wingtip","The maximum distance between the mean camber line and the chord","The locus of points halfway between the upper and lower surfaces"], a:0, r:"The chordline is the straight line through the leading and trailing edges. The halfway locus between surfaces is the mean camber line, the maximum distance between the MCL and chordline is camber, and wingtip-to-wingtip length is wingspan.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.14"},
    {q:"Angle of attack is the angle between the:", o:["Flight path and the horizon","Longitudinal axis and the chordline of the wing","Relative wind and the chordline of the airfoil","Longitudinal axis and the horizon"], a:2, r:"AOA is measured from the relative wind to the chordline. The longitudinal axis versus the horizon is pitch attitude, and the longitudinal axis versus the chordline is the angle of incidence.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.23"},
    {q:"Movement around the longitudinal axis is called:", o:["Slip","Roll","Pitch","Yaw"], a:1, r:"The longitudinal axis runs nose to tail, and movement around it is roll. Pitch occurs around the lateral (wingtip-to-wingtip) axis and yaw around the vertical axis.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.25"},
    {q:"Lift is the component of the aerodynamic force that acts:", o:["Directly opposite the thrust vector","Perpendicular to the relative wind","Parallel to the relative wind","Perpendicular to the longitudinal axis"], a:1, r:"Lift acts perpendicular to the relative wind; drag is the component parallel to the relative wind. Lift primarily opposes weight, not thrust, and it is referenced to the relative wind rather than to any aircraft axis.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.28"},
    {q:"According to the lift equation, an increase in which of the following will increase lift?", o:["Altitude","Air temperature","Humidity","Air density"], a:3, r:"L = 1/2 rho V^2 S CL: increases in density, velocity, or surface area increase lift. Higher altitude, higher humidity, and higher temperature all reduce air density, which reduces lift.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.30"},
    {q:"Total drag is composed of:", o:["Form drag and interference drag","Parasite drag and induced drag","Induced drag and skin friction","Friction drag and form drag"], a:1, r:"DT = DP + DI. Form and friction drag are the two components within parasite drag only — neither pairing by itself makes up total drag.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.39"},
    {q:"The two basic elements of parasite drag are:", o:["Wave drag and interference drag","Form drag and friction drag","Induced drag and form drag","Profile drag and induced drag"], a:1, r:"Parasite drag is not associated with the production of lift and consists of form drag (disrupted streamline flow) and the resistance of skin friction. Induced drag is the separate, lift-dependent type of drag.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.35"},
    {q:"Which type of drag is caused by airflow separating from a surface and creating a low-pressure wake behind the object?", o:["Form drag","Induced drag","Friction drag","Interference drag"], a:0, r:"Form drag results from laminar airflow separation creating a low-pressure wake that pulls the object backward. Friction drag comes from skin friction due to viscosity, and induced drag is a byproduct of lift production.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.35"},
    {q:"Induced drag is the portion of total drag associated with:", o:["Skin friction","Engine thrust","The production of lift","The disruption of streamline flow by the fuselage"], a:2, r:"Induced drag is inherent whenever the wing produces lift — wingtip vortices and downwash incline the total lift vector aft, and its horizontal component is induced drag. Skin friction and flow disruption belong to parasite drag.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.36"},
    {q:"Which devices reduce induced drag by impeding the spanwise airflow around the wingtip?", o:["Winglets, wingtip tanks, and missile rails","Flaps, slats, and spoilers","Vortex generators and rivet heads","Stall strips and trim tabs"], a:0, r:"Winglets, wingtip tanks, and missile rails block the spanwise flow that curls around the wingtip to form vortices. Flaps change camber, stall strips shape the stall pattern, and trim tabs relieve control forces — none of these target induced drag.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.38"},
    {q:"The lift-to-drag ratio (L/D) is used to determine:", o:["The stalling angle of attack","The efficiency of an airfoil","The maximum amount of lift an airfoil can produce","The efficiency of the engine"], a:1, r:"A high L/D indicates a more efficient airfoil — it is the efficiency of the wing, not the engine. L/DMAX is the greatest ratio of lift to drag, which is not the same as the maximum lift (that occurs at CLmax).", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.41"},
    {q:"The boundary layer is the layer of airflow over a surface that demonstrates local airflow retardation due to:", o:["Compressibility","Viscosity","Static pressure","Gravity"], a:1, r:"Viscosity — the air's tendency to stick to a surface — slows the airflow immediately next to the skin. The layer is only about 1 mm thick at the leading edge and grows as it moves aft.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.1"},
    {q:"The two types of airflow found within the boundary layer are:", o:["Favorable and adverse","Laminar and turbulent","Steady and unsteady","Chordwise and spanwise"], a:1, r:"The boundary layer is composed of laminar flow (smooth streamlines) and turbulent flow (disorganized, irregular). Chordwise/spanwise describe flow direction over the whole wing, and favorable/adverse describe pressure gradients, not boundary layer flow types.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.2"},
    {q:"A stall is a condition of flight in which an increase in angle of attack results in:", o:["A decrease in the coefficient of drag","An increase in the coefficient of lift","A decrease in the coefficient of lift","No change in the coefficient of lift"], a:2, r:"Beyond CLmax AOA, any further increase in AOA decreases CL — that is the definition of a stall. Below the stall region, increasing AOA increases CL.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.4"},
    {q:"The only cause of a stall is:", o:["Insufficient airspeed","Excessive angle of attack","Excessive pitch attitude","An idle power setting"], a:1, r:"A stall occurs only when AOA exceeds CLmax AOA. Airspeed, pitch attitude, and power influence when that AOA is reached, but none of them by itself causes the stall — an airplane can stall at any airspeed or attitude.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.4"},
    {q:"The only action necessary for stall recovery is to:", o:["Extend the flaps","Add full power","Level the wings","Decrease the angle of attack below CLmax AOA"], a:3, r:"Since excessive AOA is the only cause of a stall, reducing AOA below CLmax AOA is the only necessary recovery action. Power, bank, and flaps do not remove the stalled condition by themselves.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.4"},
    {q:"An airplane flying straight and level at CLmax AOA is flying at:", o:["Maximum range airspeed","Maximum endurance airspeed","Stall speed","Maximum glide airspeed"], a:2, r:"The minimum airspeed required for level flight occurs at CLmax AOA, and that minimum true airspeed is the definition of stall speed. Maximum range is flown at L/DMAX AOA and maximum endurance at a slightly greater AOA — both well below CLmax AOA.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.6"},
    {q:"Which two conditions are both required for an airplane to enter a spin?", o:["The airplane must be stalled and the ailerons deflected","Yaw must be present and the flaps must be extended","One wing must be low and power must be at idle","The airplane must be stalled and yaw must be present"], a:3, r:"A spin requires a stall plus yaw; without both, the airplane will not spin. Bank angle, power setting, aileron position, and flaps are not the required conditions.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.8"},
    {q:"To recover from a spin, the pilot must:", o:["Apply aileron opposite the spin and add power","Pull back on the stick and apply rudder into the spin","Reduce the AOA to break the stall and apply rudder opposite the yaw","Extend the flaps and reduce power"], a:2, r:"Spin recovery corrects the two factors causing the spin: reduce AOA to recover from the stall and use opposite rudder to correct the yaw. Ailerons, power, and flaps are not the recovery controls, and pulling aft stick deepens the stall.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.10"},
    {q:"The minimum airspeed for takeoff is:", o:["30 percent above the power-off stall speed","10 percent above the power-on stall speed","Equal to the power-off stall speed","20 percent above the power-off stall speed"], a:3, r:"Minimum takeoff speed is 20 percent above power-off stall speed. The 30 percent margin belongs to landing speed. Taking off at stall speed would leave no safety margin at all.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.42"},
    {q:"Landing speed is ___ percent above stall speed.", o:["20","10","50","30"], a:3, r:"Landing speed carries a 30 percent margin above stall speed because of operation at low altitude with a low power setting. The takeoff margin is the smaller 20 percent figure.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.42"},
    {q:"The members of the '4-H Club' — the worst conditions for takeoff and landing — are:", o:["High, hot, heavy, and humid","High, hazy, heavy, and hot","Hot, humid, high, and windy","Heavy, humid, hazy, and hard-surfaced"], a:0, r:"High (elevation), hot, heavy, and humid. Elevation, temperature, and humidity all decrease air density, and weight raises the required takeoff speed — together they extend takeoff and landing distances. Haze, wind, and runway surface are not 4-H members.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"Flying at maximum angle of climb yields:", o:["Minimum fuel burned during the climb","Maximum altitude gained for minimum horizontal distance traveled","Maximum ground speed while climbing","Maximum altitude gained in minimum time"], a:1, r:"Angle of climb compares altitude gained to distance traveled — used to clear obstacles off a short field. Gaining the most altitude in the least time is maximum rate of climb, a different profile.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.44"},
    {q:"Flying at maximum rate of climb yields:", o:["The maximum possible airspeed in a climb","The minimum power required for a climb","Maximum altitude gained in a minimum amount of time","Maximum altitude gained for minimum distance traveled"], a:2, r:"Rate of climb compares altitude gained to the time needed — used to expedite a climb to an assigned altitude. Altitude versus distance is angle of climb.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.44"},
    {q:"Maximum endurance is defined as:", o:["The maximum time an airplane can remain airborne on a given amount of fuel","The maximum time an airplane can spend at its cruise altitude","The farthest distance an airplane can fly with a 50-knot headwind","The maximum distance traveled over the ground for a given amount of fuel"], a:0, r:"Endurance is about time aloft on a given amount of fuel — the slower the fuel burn, the longer the flight. Distance over the ground for a given amount of fuel defines maximum range.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.45"},
    {q:"Maximum range is defined as:", o:["The maximum distance traveled over the ground for a given amount of fuel","The maximum time airborne on a given amount of fuel","The farthest distance an airplane is allowed to travel from base","The distance flown at redline airspeed on full tanks"], a:0, r:"Range is distance over the ground per amount of fuel. Time airborne defines endurance, and neither clearance limits nor redline airspeed have anything to do with the definition.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.45"},
    {q:"To achieve maximum glide range after a complete engine failure, a pilot should fly at:", o:["CLmax AOA","Redline airspeed","L/DMAX AOA","An AOA greater than L/DMAX AOA"], a:2, r:"Max glide range means a minimum angle of descent, and drag is the enemy — so fly L/DMAX AOA for minimum total drag. An AOA greater than L/DMAX AOA gives maximum glide endurance (minimum sink), CLmax AOA is the stall, and high speed wastes altitude to parasite drag.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.48"},
    {q:"The region of reverse command is found at velocities:", o:["Below maximum endurance airspeed","Above maximum range airspeed","Above maximum endurance airspeed","Above L/DMAX airspeed"], a:0, r:"Velocities below maximum endurance define the region of reverse command; velocities above it are the region of normal command. L/DMAX and max range velocities both lie in the normal command region.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.49"},
    {q:"In the region of normal command, velocity and throttle setting for level flight are:", o:["Both held constant","Inversely related","Directly related","Unrelated"], a:2, r:"In normal command, flying faster in equilibrium requires more throttle and flying slower requires less — a direct relationship. The inverse relationship is the hallmark of the region of reverse command.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.50"},
    {q:"A headwind will ___ landing distance because it ___.", o:["Have no effect; wind only matters for takeoff","Decrease; increases the indicated stall speed","Decrease; reduces ground speed","Increase; increases ground speed"], a:2, r:"A headwind reduces the ground speed at touchdown, shortening the landing rollout. It does not change the indicated stall speed, and wind affects both takeoff and landing distances.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"On the turn-and-slip indicator, the aircraft is in coordinated flight when:", o:["The turn needle is centered","The ball is displaced toward the inside of the turn","The ball is centered","The ball is displaced toward the outside of the turn"], a:2, r:"A centered ball means coordinated flight. Ball displaced in the same direction as the turn indicates a slip; opposite the turn indicates a skid. The turn needle shows turn direction and rate, not coordination.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.51"},
    {q:"A slip is caused by:", o:["Too much rudder in the desired direction of turn","Excessive power at low airspeed","Opposite or insufficient rudder in the desired direction of turn","Excessive aileron deflection alone"], a:2, r:"A slip results from opposite or insufficient rudder, with the yaw toward the outside of the turn and the ball deflected toward the inside. Too much rudder in the direction of turn produces a skid instead.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.51"},
    {q:"A skid is caused by:", o:["A stalled inside wing","Too little bank angle for the airspeed","Too much rudder in the desired direction of turn","Opposite or insufficient rudder in the desired direction of turn"], a:2, r:"A skid results from using too much rudder in the direction of the turn, yawing toward the inside with the ball thrown outside by centrifugal force. Insufficient rudder gives a slip, and a stalled wing with yaw defines a spin, not a skid.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.52"},
    {q:"During a high-power, high-AOA climb in a propeller airplane, P-factor will yaw the nose to the:", o:["Left, because the slipstream strikes the left wing","Right, because the up-going blade creates more thrust","Left, because the down-going blade on the right side creates more thrust","Right, because of gyroscopic precession"], a:2, r:"With the relative wind below the thrust line, each blade meets the relative wind at a different angle; the down-going blade on the right side produces more thrust, yawing the nose left. The up-going blade produces less thrust, and slipstream and precession are separate effects.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.53"},
    {q:"Load factor (n) is defined as the ratio of:", o:["Total lift to the airplane's weight","Weight to wing surface area","Thrust to drag","Vertical lift to horizontal lift"], a:0, r:"n = L / W, measured in Gs — the number of times the earth's gravitational pull felt by the pilot. Weight over wing area is wing loading, a different ratio.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.54"},
    {q:"The greatest load factor an airplane can sustain without any risk of permanent deformation is the:", o:["Ultimate load factor","Elastic limit","Limit load factor","Maneuver point"], a:2, r:"The limit load factor is the maximum anticipated in normal daily operations, with no risk of permanent deformation. The ultimate load factor is where structural failure becomes imminent, the elastic limit applies to individual components, and the maneuver point is a location on the V-n diagram.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.55"},
    {q:"The ultimate load factor is ___ of the limit load factor.", o:["150 percent","75 percent","200 percent","110 percent"], a:0, r:"Ultimate load factor is 150 percent (1.5 times) the limit load factor. Exceeding it makes structural failure imminent — something major on the airplane will break.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.55"},
    {q:"Redline airspeed (VNE) is:", o:["The lowest airspeed at which the limit load factor can be reached","The stall speed at the limit load factor","The recommended turbulent air penetration airspeed","The highest airspeed an airplane is allowed to fly"], a:3, r:"VNE (velocity never-to-exceed) is the highest allowable airspeed; flight above it can cause structural damage. The lowest airspeed at which the limit load can be reached — and the turbulence penetration speed — is maneuver speed, on the opposite side of the envelope.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.56"},
    {q:"The greatest wake turbulence vortex strength is produced by a generating airplane that is:", o:["Heavy, fast, and in landing configuration","Heavy, slow, and clean","Light, slow, and with flaps extended","Heavy, slow, and with flaps fully extended"], a:1, r:"Heavy weight demands more lift (greater tip pressure differential), slow speed means dominant induced drag, and a clean wing concentrates lift outboard. Lowering flaps shifts lift toward the root and actually weakens the tip vortex, so a flaps-extended airplane is not the worst case.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.57"},
    {q:"Wind shear is defined as:", o:["A sudden change in wind direction and/or speed over a short distance in the atmosphere","Any surface wind stronger than 20 knots","The corkscrewing air behind a propeller","The turbulence generated by wingtip vortices"], a:0, r:"Wind shear is a sudden change in wind direction and/or speed over a short distance, abruptly changing aircraft performance. Corkscrewing propwash is slipstream swirl, and vortex turbulence is wake turbulence — different hazards.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.59"}
  ]
},
{
  name: "Test 2 — Medium",
  desc: "Applied relationships and cause-and-effect with plausible near-miss distractors.",
  questions: [
    {q:"An airplane in trimmed flight is ___ in equilibrium flight.", o:["Always — trimmed flight and equilibrium flight are the same condition","Only sometimes — trim requires zero net moments, but equilibrium also requires zero net force","Never — a trimmed airplane must be accelerating","Only when it is on the ground"], a:1, r:"Trimmed flight requires only that the sum of the moments around the CG equal zero. Equilibrium additionally requires the sum of all forces to equal zero, so a trimmed airplane may still be accelerating (for example, in a trimmed, accelerating descent). Trim is necessary for equilibrium but not sufficient.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.5"},
    {q:"As air density decreases with altitude, what happens to power, thrust, and lift?", o:["Power decreases, but thrust and lift increase","All three decrease","Thrust increases because the propeller bites less air","Only lift decreases; power and thrust are unaffected"], a:1, r:"Less dense air reduces power because the engine takes in less air, reduces thrust because the propeller is less efficient in thin air, and reduces lift because thin air exerts less force on the airfoils. All three fall together.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.2"},
    {q:"Humid air is less dense than dry air because:", o:["Water molecules displace an equal number of air molecules but have less mass","Water vapor increases the number of particles per unit volume","Water vapor heats the surrounding air","Water molecules increase the static pressure"], a:0, r:"Displacing air molecules with lighter water molecules leaves the particle count per unit volume unchanged while total mass falls, so density decreases. Humidity does not add particles, and its effect works through mass, not temperature or pressure.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.7"},
    {q:"According to the General Gas Law, if pressure remains constant, an increase in temperature must result in:", o:["An increase in pressure","A decrease in density","No change in density","An increase in density"], a:1, r:"With P = rho R T and pressure held constant, density and temperature are inversely related — warmer air at the same pressure is less dense. Density rising with temperature would violate the equation, and pressure was stipulated constant.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.8"},
    {q:"As subsonic air flows through the constricted portion of a streamtube, velocity ___ and static pressure ___.", o:["Increases; increases","Decreases; decreases","Decreases; increases","Increases; decreases"], a:3, r:"The continuity equation forces velocity up as area shrinks, raising dynamic pressure. Since total pressure is constant in a closed system (Bernoulli), static pressure must fall by the same amount. Both rising together would violate the constant total pressure.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.11"},
    {q:"Extending the flaps:", o:["Increases positive camber and increases the stalling AOA","Increases the airfoil's positive camber and decreases the stalling AOA","Decreases camber but increases the coefficient of lift","Increases the stalling AOA and steepens the takeoff attitude"], a:1, r:"Flaps change the chordline, adding positive camber and shifting the zero-lift point left — but CLmax AOA (stalling AOA) decreases. The trade-off is worthwhile because flatter takeoff and landing attitudes improve visibility.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.34"},
    {q:"Calibrated airspeed is indicated airspeed corrected for ___, and equivalent airspeed is calibrated airspeed corrected for ___.", o:["Position error; local wind","Instrument error; compressibility error","Density differences; wind","Compressibility error; instrument error"], a:1, r:"The ICE-TG chain: IAS corrected for instrument error gives CAS; CAS corrected for compressibility gives EAS; EAS corrected for density gives TAS; TAS corrected for wind gives ground speed. Reversing the first two corrections gets the chain out of order.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.12"},
    {q:"Ground speed is obtained by correcting true airspeed for:", o:["The wind component","Compressibility","Air density","Instrument error"], a:0, r:"GS = TAS plus or minus the wind component — TAS is the speed through the air mass, so correcting for the air mass's own movement yields speed over the ground. Density and compressibility corrections occur earlier in the airspeed chain.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.12"},
    {q:"The camber of an airfoil is:", o:["A line halfway between the upper and lower surfaces","The maximum distance between the mean camber line and the chordline","The distance from the leading edge to the trailing edge","The cross-sectional height of the airfoil"], a:1, r:"Camber measures the curvature: the maximum distance between the MCL and the chordline, perpendicular to the chordline. The halfway line itself is the mean camber line, leading-to-trailing-edge distance is chord, and cross-sectional height is thickness.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.15"},
    {q:"Which airflow produces lift, and why?", o:["Chordwise flow, because it is the only airflow accelerated over the wing","Spanwise flow, because it travels from root to tip","Both chordwise and spanwise flow contribute equally","Downwash, because it pushes the air downward"], a:0, r:"Only chordwise flow — at right angles to the leading edge — accelerates over the wing to create the pressure differential. Spanwise flow parallels the leading edge, is not accelerated, and produces no lift.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.18"},
    {q:"Relative wind is:", o:["Equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the flight path","Determined directly from pitch attitude","Equal in magnitude and direction to the flight path","Always aligned with the longitudinal axis"], a:0, r:"Relative wind is the airflow the airplane experiences and is always equal and opposite to the flight path. It is not tied to the longitudinal axis or pitch attitude — flight path, relative wind, and AOA should never be inferred from pitch attitude.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.22"},
    {q:"The angle of incidence is the angle between:", o:["The relative wind and the chordline","The flight path and the horizon","The airplane's longitudinal axis and the chordline of the wing","The longitudinal axis and the horizon"], a:2, r:"Angle of incidence is a fixed design angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing chordline. Relative wind to chordline is AOA, and longitudinal axis to horizon is pitch attitude.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.24"},
    {q:"On a subsonic airfoil, the aerodynamic center is located:", o:["Halfway along the mean camber line","At the trailing edge","At the center of gravity","Approximately one-quarter of the chord length aft of the leading edge"], a:3, r:"The aerodynamic center — the point about which all changes in aerodynamic force take place — sits at roughly 25 percent chord on a subsonic airfoil, at the point of maximum thickness/most positive camber. The CG is where weight is concentrated, a different point.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.27"},
    {q:"With a positive AOA, lift develops because the air flowing over the upper surface:", o:["Senses a greater reduction in streamtube area, accelerates, and its static pressure drops below the lower surface's","Slows down, raising its static pressure above the lower surface's","Is compressed, increasing its density relative to the lower surface","Must travel a longer path and arrive at the trailing edge at the same time"], a:0, r:"Per the continuity equation the constricted upper streamtube accelerates the flow, and per Bernoulli the higher dynamic pressure means lower static pressure on top — the pressure imbalance is lift. The equal-transit-time story is not the Student Guide's explanation, and subsonic flow is treated as incompressible.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.29"},
    {q:"To maintain level flight while increasing angle of attack, velocity must:", o:["Remain exactly constant","Decrease — velocity and AOA are inversely related in level flight","Increase — velocity and AOA rise together","First increase, then decrease"], a:1, r:"If AOA (and thus CL) increases while velocity is held, lift exceeds weight and the airplane climbs. Holding altitude requires trading velocity against AOA — an inverse relationship.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.33"},
    {q:"Induced drag will increase when:", o:["Velocity increases","Wingspan increases","Air density increases","Wingspan decreases"], a:3, r:"Induced drag is reduced by increasing density, velocity, or wingspan — so a shorter span raises induced drag. Increases in span, speed, or density all work the other way.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.37"},
    {q:"Compared with power-on stall speed, power-off stall speed is:", o:["Identical — power has no effect on stall speed","Higher, because no vertical component of thrust is helping support the airplane's weight","Lower, because idle thrust reduces total drag","Lower, because the propeller accelerates air over the wing at idle"], a:1, r:"In a power-on stall at high pitch attitude, part of the weight is carried by the vertical component of thrust, and on propeller airplanes the propwash adds lift behind the propeller — so power-on stall speed is less than power-off. That makes the power-off number the higher of the two.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.7"},
    {q:"During a spin, the outside (up-going) wing has ___ AOA and ___ drag than the inside wing.", o:["A higher; less","A lower; more","A higher; more","A lower; less"], a:3, r:"Yaw gives each wing a different average relative wind: the inside, down-going wing flies at a higher AOA (more stalled) with more drag; the outside, up-going wing is still stalled but at a lower AOA with less drag. That drag differential sustains the autorotation.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.9"},
    {q:"Doubling airspeed will increase parasite drag approximately:", o:["Four times","Two times","Sixteen times","Nine times"], a:0, r:"Profile (parasite) drag increases as the square of velocity at subsonic speeds: doubling airspeed quadruples it, and tripling airspeed increases it nine times. A simple doubling understates the effect.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.35"},
    {q:"As airspeed decreases in level flight, induced drag:", o:["Decreases, because dynamic pressure decreases","Increases only if the flaps are extended","Increases, because a greater AOA is required to produce lift equal to weight","Remains constant as long as altitude is constant"], a:2, r:"The slower the airspeed, the greater the AOA needed to support the airplane's weight — creating more violent vortices, more downwash, and more induced drag. Induced drag varies inversely with airspeed regardless of flap position.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.37"},
    {q:"At L/DMAX AOA:", o:["Parasite drag is zero","Parasite drag and induced drag are equal","Lift is at its maximum value","Induced drag is zero"], a:1, r:"L/DMAX sits at the bottom of the total drag curve, where parasite and induced drag are equal and total drag is minimum. Neither drag type is ever zero in flight, and maximum lift occurs at CLmax AOA, not L/DMAX AOA.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.41"},
    {q:"At velocities below L/DMAX the airplane is affected primarily by ___ drag; above L/DMAX, primarily by ___ drag.", o:["Induced; parasite","Form; friction","Parasite; induced","Friction; induced"], a:0, r:"Induced drag dominates at low speed (high AOA), and parasite drag — which grows with the square of velocity — dominates at high speed. Form and friction are merely the two components of parasite drag.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.41"},
    {q:"Compared with a laminar boundary layer, a turbulent boundary layer:", o:["Produces more friction drag but adheres better, delaying separation","Is thinner and exists only at the leading edge","Produces less friction drag and separates more easily","Produces less friction drag and delays separation"], a:0, r:"Turbulent flow costs more friction drag but its higher energy keeps it attached to the surface longer. Laminar flow is the low-friction, easily separated one, and the boundary layer actually grows thicker moving aft — the thin leading-edge layer is laminar.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.2"},
    {q:"The adverse pressure gradient on an airfoil is found:", o:["From the point of maximum thickness aft toward the trailing edge","From the leading edge to the point of maximum thickness","Only on the lower surface","Only when the flaps are extended"], a:0, r:"Flowing from the low static pressure at maximum thickness toward the higher static pressure at the trailing edge, the air faces increasing pressure that impedes the boundary layer — the adverse gradient, strongest at high AOA. The leading-edge-to-max-thickness run is the favorable gradient.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.3"},
    {q:"Boundary layer separation results in a stall when:", o:["The separation point moves far enough forward that net suction on top of the airfoil decreases, decreasing CL","The favorable pressure gradient becomes too strong","Spanwise flow stops at the wingtip","The boundary layer transitions from laminar to turbulent flow"], a:0, r:"When the lower levels of the boundary layer stagnate against the adverse gradient, the layer separates and flow reverses behind the separation point; if that point moves close enough to the leading edge, suction — and CL — collapse into a stall. Laminar-to-turbulent transition is normal, and a favorable gradient helps the boundary layer.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.3"},
    {q:"When an airplane burns off fuel or drops ordnance, its stall speed:", o:["Increases, because the wing loading increases","Does not change — stall speed depends only on the airfoil","Decreases, because less lift is required to maintain level flight","Decreases, because CLmax increases"], a:2, r:"Lower weight means less required lift, which can be generated at a lower velocity — stall speed falls. This is why carrier pilots dump fuel before shipboard landings. CLmax AOA is unchanged; it is the required lift, not the airfoil's capability, that changed.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.6"},
    {q:"As altitude increases, true stall speed ___ and indicated stall speed ___.", o:["Increases; increases","Increases; remains the same","Remains the same; increases","Decreases; remains the same"], a:1, r:"At altitude the airplane meets fewer air molecules, so a higher TAS is needed to build the same dynamic pressure — true stall speed rises. Because the sea-level density term in the indicated stall speed equation is constant, indicated stall speed does not change with altitude.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.5"},
    {q:"Flying within approximately one wingspan of the ground (ground effect):", o:["Increases induced drag and the thrust required","Directly decreases parasite drag","Decreases induced drag and reduces the thrust required at low speed","Increases the downwash behind the wing"], a:2, r:"Ground effect weakens the wingtip vortices, reducing downwash so the lift vector rotates forward — induced drag and low-speed thrust required both drop. It has no direct effect on parasite drag, and excess speed in the flare can cause a considerable float.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.37"},
    {q:"Rough surfaces and protruding rivet heads primarily increase which type of drag?", o:["Friction drag","Induced drag","Form drag","Interference drag"], a:0, r:"Roughness thickens the boundary layer and creates greater skin friction — minimized with a glossy, flat finish and by eliminating protruding rivet heads. Form drag concerns the object's overall shape and separation wake, not surface texture.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.35"},
    {q:"Landing distance will decrease when:", o:["Altitude increases","Humidity decreases","Weight increases","Temperature increases"], a:1, r:"Lower humidity means denser air, a lower landing velocity, and a shorter rollout. Higher altitude and temperature reduce density (raising landing velocity), and higher weight demands more airspeed to stay airborne — all three lengthen the landing distance.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"Maximum endurance AOA is ___ CLmax AOA.", o:["Equal to","Greater than","Unrelated to","Less than"], a:3, r:"Maximum endurance is flown at the bottom of the power curve — slower than L/DMAX, so its AOA is greater than L/DMAX AOA, but it is still an equilibrium airspeed above stall speed, so its AOA remains below the stalling (CLmax) AOA.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.46"},
    {q:"On the power curve, maximum endurance is found:", o:["Where a straight line from the origin is tangent to the curve","At the bottom of the curve, where fuel flow is minimum","At the highest point of the curve","At the intersection with redline airspeed"], a:1, r:"Fuel flow varies directly with power output, so minimum fuel flow — and maximum time aloft — occurs at the very bottom of the power required curve. The origin-tangent point is maximum range, a faster airspeed.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.46"},
    {q:"For a propeller airplane, maximum range is found:", o:["Where a straight line drawn from the origin is tangent to the power curve","At CLmax AOA","At a velocity slower than maximum endurance","At the bottom of the power curve"], a:0, r:"Maximum range minimizes fuel flow per unit of velocity; the smallest such ratio that keeps the airplane airborne is the origin-tangent point on the power curve — L/DMAX AOA and velocity. The bottom of the curve is maximum endurance, which is slower, and CLmax AOA is the stall.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.46"},
    {q:"As altitude increases, maximum range and maximum endurance performance:", o:["Both decrease","Range increases while endurance decreases","Both increase","Remain unchanged"], a:2, r:"Colder air at altitude makes turbine engines more fuel efficient, so fuel flow drops even though the throttle is physically advanced. Less fuel burned per hour improves endurance, and a higher TAS on less fuel improves range — both benefit.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.47"},
    {q:"Maximum glide endurance requires flying:", o:["Exactly at L/DMAX velocity","Slower than L/DMAX velocity, at an AOA greater than L/DMAX AOA","Faster than L/DMAX velocity","At CLmax AOA"], a:1, r:"Minimizing rate of descent means flying at the minimum power deficit — a velocity below L/DMAX with a correspondingly higher AOA. L/DMAX itself gives maximum glide range (distance), and CLmax AOA is the stall, not a glide profile.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.48"},
    {q:"In the region of reverse command, velocity and throttle setting for level flight are:", o:["Fixed by the flap setting","Directly related — a slower airspeed requires less throttle","Inversely related — stabilizing at a slower airspeed requires more throttle","Unrelated — throttle only controls climb rate"], a:2, r:"Below maximum endurance airspeed, the slower you fly the higher the AOA and induced drag, so more throttle is needed to hold level flight. The direct relationship describes the region of normal command.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.50"},
    {q:"Increasing density altitude on takeoff will:", o:["Raise the indicated takeoff airspeed","Shorten the takeoff roll because thin air means less drag","Increase the thrust available from the engine","Raise the required takeoff velocity and distance while indicated takeoff airspeed stays constant"], a:3, r:"Higher DA (lower density) requires a higher true takeoff velocity, decreases engine thrust, slows acceleration, and lengthens the roll — yet the indicated airspeed for takeoff remains the same regardless of temperature, humidity, and elevation.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"Aerodynamic braking slows the airplane by:", o:["Holding a constant pitch attitude after touchdown to expose more surface to the relative wind, increasing parasite drag","Increasing induced drag with full aft stick at high speed","Raising the flaps to increase lift over the wing","Applying maximum wheel braking at touchdown speed"], a:0, r:"Aerodynamic braking, used at the beginning of the landing roll, increases parasite drag by exposing more of the airplane to the relative wind, reducing brake wear. Mechanical (wheel) braking comes later, after enough weight has transferred to the wheels.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"During a crosswind takeoff, directional control is primarily maintained with the ___, while the ailerons are placed into the wind to ___.", o:["Rudder; overcome the lateral stability that is trying to roll the airplane","Rudder; add lift to the upwind wing","Nosewheel; increase rudder effectiveness","Ailerons; maintain directional control"], a:0, r:"The rudder is the primary directional control (with the nosewheel assisting until the rudder becomes effective at higher speed). Ailerons into the wind are not for steering — they counter the rolling tendency produced by the airplane's lateral stability.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"Taking off from a short airfield surrounded by high obstacles such as trees or power lines calls for:", o:["A maximum endurance profile","A maximum rate of climb profile","A cruise climb at redline airspeed","A maximum angle of climb profile"], a:3, r:"Obstacle clearance demands the most altitude gained in the least horizontal distance — maximum angle of climb. Maximum rate of climb trades ground distance for time and is used to expedite a climb to an assigned altitude instead.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.44"},
    {q:"In a slip, turn radius will ___ and turn rate will ___.", o:["Increase; decrease","Decrease; increase","Decrease; decrease","Increase; increase"], a:0, r:"With insufficient rudder, the slip yaws toward the outside of the turn: radius grows and rate drops. Radius shrinking while rate grows is the signature of a skid, the opposite condition.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.51"},
    {q:"A stall entered from a skidding turn will cause the airplane to:", o:["Roll inverted","Pitch straight ahead without rolling","Enter a spiral rather than a stall","Roll toward wings level"], a:0, r:"A skidded turn stall rolls the airplane inverted and will probably be fatal at low altitude. A stall from a slip is the safer case — the airplane rolls toward wings level.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.52"},
    {q:"The balance ball is displaced to the right. The proper correction is to:", o:["Apply right rudder — 'step on the ball'","Reduce power","Apply left rudder","Increase right bank"], a:0, r:"Uncoordinated flight is corrected by applying rudder in the direction the ball is displaced — the 'step on the ball' rule. Rudder away from the ball worsens the condition, and bank or power changes do not restore coordination.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.51"},
    {q:"P-factor has a noticeable effect only when:", o:["Flying at cruise AOA at high airspeed","The propeller is windmilling","The engine is at idle and the AOA is low","The engine is at high power and the thrust axis is displaced from the relative wind"], a:3, r:"Both requirements must be met: a high power setting and a thrust axis displaced from the relative wind. Designers align the thrust axis with the relative wind at cruise, so P-factor shows up at AOAs well away from cruise — such as high-AOA climbs.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.53"},
    {q:"Slipstream swirl yaws the nose left because the corkscrewing propwash:", o:["Reduces thrust on the right half of the propeller disk","Blows the rudder to the left","Increases the AOA on the vertical stabilizer, creating a lifting force that pulls the tail right","Strikes the left wing and increases its lift"], a:2, r:"The corkscrew flow wraps around the fuselage and meets the vertical stabilizer at an increased AOA, generating a horizontal lifting force that pulls the tail right and the nose left — most pronounced at high power and low airspeed. Right rudder and lateral stick compensate. Blade thrust asymmetry is P-factor, a separate effect.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.53"},
    {q:"In a level, constant-airspeed turn, altitude is maintained by:", o:["Increasing total lift so its vertical component continues to equal weight","Holding the same total lift as in wings-level flight","Letting the horizontal lift component oppose weight","Reducing the load factor below one"], a:0, r:"In a bank the lift vector splits into vertical and horizontal components, and only the vertical component opposes weight. The pilot must increase total lift (normally by pulling back to raise AOA) or the airplane descends; keeping wings-level lift is not enough, which is why load factor rises above one.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.54"},
    {q:"After an overstress/over-G, the airplane must be inspected because:", o:["The limit load factor automatically resets after landing","Internal components such as hydraulic actuators and engine mounts may fail before the airframe shows any damage","Overstress damage is always visible on the skin","The wings will have departed the airplane"], a:1, r:"Overstress may occur without visibly damaging the airframe, but components not built to airframe strength — hydraulic actuators, engine mounts — can break first (a failed engine mount can spill fuel onto hot casings). Every overstress must be reported to maintenance.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.55"},
    {q:"Maneuver speed (Va) is:", o:["The lowest airspeed at which the limit load factor can be reached","The highest airspeed the airplane may ever fly","The speed at which parasite and induced drag are equal","The one-G stall speed"], a:0, r:"Va, or cornering velocity, marks the maneuver point where the accelerated stall line meets the limit load line — the slowest speed at which the limit load can be generated. The never-exceed speed is redline, and equal parasite/induced drag defines L/DMAX.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.56"},
    {q:"Wingtip vortices sink at ___ and level off approximately ___ below the generating airplane's flight path.", o:["1,000 feet per minute; 2,500 feet","400 to 500 feet per minute; 2,500 feet","400 to 500 feet per minute; 900 feet","100 feet per minute; 400 feet"], a:2, r:"Vortices sink at 400 to 500 fpm and level off about 900 feet below the generating aircraft's flight path, breaking up after a few minutes. The 2,500-foot figure is the parallel-runway separation caution, not the vortex sink depth.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.57"},
    {q:"Wind shear is most hazardous:", o:["At cruise altitude with ample airspeed","Only when flying in icing conditions","During takeoffs and landings, at slow airspeed and low altitude","Only above maneuvering speed"], a:2, r:"With ample airspeed and altitude, wind shear is not a serious threat — once stabilized in the new air mass the airplane behaves normally. The danger is a sudden performance change at slow speed close to the ground, compounded by an overly aggressive or passive pilot response.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.60"}
  ]
},
{
  name: "Test 3 — Hard",
  desc: "NOT-style stems, applied flight scenarios, and near-miss distractors.",
  questions: [
    {q:"An airplane in equilibrium flight is ___ in trimmed flight.", o:["Always — equilibrium requires zero net moments, which is exactly the trim condition","Never — equilibrium and trim are mutually exclusive","Only when straight and level","Only sometimes — equilibrium concerns forces, not moments"], a:0, r:"Equilibrium requires both the sum of forces and the sum of moments about the CG to be zero. Since trimmed flight requires only zero moments, every equilibrium condition automatically satisfies trim. The reverse is not true — a trimmed airplane may still have unbalanced forces. Equilibrium also includes stabilized climbs and descents, not just level flight.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.4"},
    {q:"Which of the following is NOT required for an airplane to be in equilibrium flight?", o:["The sum of all moments around the center of gravity must equal zero","The absence of both linear and angular acceleration","The sum of all forces must equal zero","All four forces must be equal in magnitude"], a:3, r:"Equilibrium means the opposing forces cancel each other — not that all four forces share one magnitude (lift/weight and thrust/drag pairs generally differ from each other). Zero net force, zero net moment, and no acceleration are all genuine requirements.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.4"},
    {q:"Which statement about steady airflow is TRUE?", o:["The velocity of every particle passing a given point is always the same, though velocity may differ between points","Every particle in the flow has identical speed and direction everywhere in the flow","Static pressure at a fixed point varies continuously with time","Particles routinely cross from one streamline to another"], a:0, r:"In steady airflow, conditions at each point are constant over time, and each particle follows the path of the preceding particle — but speed and direction may differ from one point in the flow to another. Particles never cross streamlines, and time-varying pressure at a point would make the flow unsteady.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.9"},
    {q:"A pressure cooker raises pressure by holding ___ constant and increasing ___.", o:["Temperature; density","Density; temperature","Volume; humidity","Pressure; temperature"], a:1, r:"Per the General Gas Law (P = rho R T), keeping density constant while raising temperature raises pressure — the Student Guide's pressure cooker example. The other pairings either reverse the roles or involve humidity, which is not a gas law term.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.8"},
    {q:"As air accelerates through the narrowest part of a streamtube, total pressure:", o:["Converts entirely into dynamic pressure","Decreases as the static pressure falls","Remains constant while dynamic pressure rises and static pressure falls","Increases along with the dynamic pressure"], a:2, r:"A streamtube is a closed system: total pressure stays constant, so the rise in dynamic pressure is exactly offset by a fall in static pressure. Total pressure neither rises, falls, nor becomes purely dynamic — static pressure never reaches zero in this exchange.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.11"},
    {q:"True airspeed will equal indicated airspeed only:", o:["Whenever ground speed equals true airspeed","At the altitude where the lapse rate stops","Whenever the air is perfectly calm","At sea level under standard day conditions"], a:3, r:"The pitot-static system is calibrated for standard sea level density, so TAS = IAS only there. Calm wind makes ground speed equal TAS — a different pair — and the isothermal layer near 36,000 feet has nothing to do with the IAS/TAS relationship.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.12"},
    {q:"A symmetric airfoil at zero angle of attack produces:", o:["Lift, because the upper surface is always more curved","Negative lift, due to downwash","Lift equal to its weight","No lift, because the mean camber line is coincident with the chordline"], a:3, r:"With the MCL on the chordline there is no camber, so at zero AOA the pressure distributions above and below are identical — no lift. It is the positively cambered airfoil that produces lift at zero AOA.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.15"},
    {q:"Wing area (S) is the area within the outline of the wing in the plane of its chord:", o:["Excluding the area covered by the fuselage","Measured perpendicular to the chord plane","Including the area within the fuselage, hull, or nacelles","Counting only the exposed wing panels"], a:2, r:"By definition, wing area includes the area inside the fuselage, hull, or nacelles — not just the exposed panels. Excluding the fuselage section is the intuitive but incorrect reading.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.17"},
    {q:"Flight path, relative wind, and angle of attack should NEVER be inferred from:", o:["The airspeed indicator","Pitch attitude","Wing camber","The turn needle"], a:1, r:"Pitch attitude is only the angle between the longitudinal axis and the horizon; the flight path (and its opposite, the relative wind) can point somewhere quite different — an airplane can descend with its nose level. The Student Guide states this caution explicitly for pitch attitude.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.20"},
    {q:"As fuel burns, ordnance is expended, or cargo is loaded and unloaded, the center of gravity:", o:["Remains fixed at the manufacturer's datum","Can shift, because its location depends on the distribution of weight","Moves to coincide with the aerodynamic center","Always migrates aft"], a:1, r:"The CG is the point where all weight is considered concentrated, so redistributing weight moves it. It is measured from the datum but is not the datum, it is distinct from the aerodynamic center, and the direction of shift depends on what was moved or expended.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.26"},
    {q:"The aerodynamic center differs from the center of gravity in that the aerodynamic center is the point:", o:["About which the airplane would balance if suspended","Along the chordline where all changes in aerodynamic force take place","Through which the three axes pass","Where all of the airplane's weight is considered concentrated"], a:1, r:"The aerodynamic center — about 25 percent chord on a subsonic airfoil — is where changes in aerodynamic force act. Concentrated weight, the balance point, and the intersection of the three axes all describe the center of gravity.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.27"},
    {q:"Increasing the angle of attack increases lift because:", o:["Total pressure above the wing increases","The streamtube above the wing is constricted more, so velocity rises and static pressure over the upper surface falls further","Air density above the wing increases with AOA","More air is deflected spanwise toward the wingtips"], a:1, r:"At a higher positive AOA the air over the top senses a larger reduction in streamtube area; continuity accelerates it and Bernoulli drops its static pressure, widening the pressure imbalance. Density is unchanged, spanwise flow produces no lift, and total pressure stays constant.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.29"},
    {q:"If velocity doubles while all other lift equation factors remain constant, lift becomes:", o:["Eight times greater","Twice as great","Unchanged, because CL falls automatically","Four times greater"], a:3, r:"Velocity is squared in L = 1/2 rho V^2 S CL, so doubling V quadruples lift. CL does not change on its own — it depends on AOA and camber, which were held constant.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.30"},
    {q:"Which pair of coefficient-of-lift factors can the pilot directly control?", o:["Aspect ratio and air density","Angle of attack and camber (via the flaps)","Wing surface area and altitude","Viscosity and compressibility"], a:1, r:"Of the factors inside CL, only AOA (the most important and easiest to change) and airfoil shape/camber are controllable. The pilot has no control over viscosity or compressibility, aspect ratio is fixed on modern non-swing-wing aircraft, and density is set by the atmosphere.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.31"},
    {q:"Which of the following is NOT an effect of extending the flaps?", o:["An increase in the stalling angle of attack","An increase in the airfoil's positive camber","A decrease in CLmax AOA","Flatter takeoff and landing attitudes that improve visibility"], a:0, r:"Flaps shift the zero-lift point left and the stalling AOA (CLmax AOA) decreases — it does not increase. The genuine effects are added positive camber, a reduced stalling AOA, and improved visibility from flatter attitudes.", ref:"Ch 1, EO 2.34"},
    {q:"Induced drag will increase when:", o:["Air temperature increases","Wingspan increases","Humidity decreases","Altitude decreases"], a:0, r:"Warmer air is less dense, and induced drag is reduced by increasing density — so lower density raises it. Decreasing humidity and decreasing altitude both increase density (reducing induced drag), and a longer wingspan also reduces it.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.37"},
    {q:"As velocity increases, total drag:", o:["Always decreases as induced drag falls","Remains constant in level flight","Cannot be determined without knowing the airspeed relative to L/DMAX","Always increases with the square of velocity"], a:2, r:"Total drag is the sum of two opposing trends: below L/DMAX, accelerating reduces total drag because induced drag falls sharply; above L/DMAX, accelerating raises total drag as parasite drag grows with the square of velocity. Without knowing which side of L/DMAX you are on, the direction of change is indeterminate.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.40"},
    {q:"Which of the following will NOT reduce induced drag?", o:["Increasing wingspan","Increasing velocity","Installing winglets or wingtip tanks","Climbing to a higher altitude"], a:3, r:"Higher altitude means lower density, which increases induced drag. Increased velocity, spanwise-flow blockers such as winglets and tip tanks, and a longer wingspan all genuinely reduce it.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.38"},
    {q:"Of the two components of parasite drag, ___ is easier to reduce by design, while ___ is the most difficult to reduce.", o:["Induced drag; form drag","Skin friction (by polishing); form drag","Friction drag; interference drag","Form drag (by streamlining); skin friction"], a:3, r:"A more streamlined shape readily reduces form drag, but no surface is perfectly smooth — skin friction is the type of parasite drag most difficult to reduce, minimized only by glossy flat finishes and eliminating protrusions. Induced drag is not a component of parasite drag at all.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.35"},
    {q:"A laminar boundary layer reduces friction drag, but its disadvantage is that it:", o:["Grows thicker than a turbulent layer","Separates from the surface more easily than a turbulent boundary layer","Produces more skin friction at high speed","Cannot form near the leading edge"], a:1, r:"Laminar flow produces very little friction but is easily separated; the turbulent layer, despite higher friction, adheres better and delays separation. Laminar flow is actually what exists at the thin leading-edge portion of the boundary layer.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.2"},
    {q:"The favorable pressure gradient extends from:", o:["The point of maximum thickness to the trailing edge","The leading edge (high static pressure) to the point of maximum thickness (low static pressure)","The wing root outward to the wingtip","The trailing edge forward to the leading edge"], a:1, r:"Air flowing from high toward low static pressure is assisted, keeping the boundary layer's kinetic energy high — that run ends at maximum thickness. Beyond it, pressure rises toward the trailing edge and the gradient becomes adverse. Root-to-tip describes spanwise flow, not a pressure gradient along the chord.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.3"},
    {q:"A carrier pilot dumps fuel before a shipboard landing in order to:", o:["Increase the indicated stall speed for a steeper approach","Reduce induced drag during the approach","Shift the center of gravity aft","Reduce stall speed and therefore approach speed"], a:3, r:"Reducing weight reduces the lift required, which lowers stall speed — and with it the approach speed, easing the arrested landing. Raising stall speed would be counterproductive, and the purpose is speed reduction, not CG or drag management.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.6"},
    {q:"Two identical airplanes at identical weights fly at 5,000 and 20,000 feet. Compared with the lower airplane, the higher airplane stalls at:", o:["The same indicated airspeed but a higher true airspeed","The same true airspeed but a higher indicated airspeed","A lower true airspeed","A higher indicated airspeed and a higher true airspeed"], a:0, r:"At altitude, fewer air molecules mean a higher TAS is required to generate the needed dynamic pressure, so true stall speed rises. Indicated stall speed does not change with altitude because the sea-level density constant is built into the indicated stall speed equation.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.5"},
    {q:"For propeller-driven airplanes, power-on stall speed is lower than power-off partly because:", o:["Engine torque raises CLmax","The spinning propeller reduces the airplane's effective weight","The portion of the wing directly behind the propeller produces more lift from the accelerated air","Idle thrust produces a large vertical component"], a:2, r:"Two effects lower the power-on stall speed: propwash accelerates air over the wing section behind the propeller (extra lift), and at high pitch attitude the vertical component of thrust supports part of the weight. Torque does not change CLmax, and idle thrust — the power-off case — offers no meaningful vertical component.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.7"},
    {q:"Autorotation in a spin is self-sustaining because the more deeply stalled inside wing generates:", o:["More drag, creating a drag differential that continues the yaw","Less drag, which accelerates the roll","More lift, which rolls the airplane upright","No aerodynamic force at all"], a:0, r:"The inside wing's higher AOA raises its coefficient of drag; the resulting drag differential keeps yawing the airplane around the spin axis, and combined roll and yaw propagate the autorotation. A stalled wing still produces (reduced) lift and substantial drag — not more lift, and not nothing.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.9"},
    {q:"During spin recovery, applying opposite rudder while holding full aft stick will:", o:["Fail to recover — both the stall and the yaw must be corrected","Recover the airplane normally","Reduce the AOA by itself","Automatically convert the spin into a climb"], a:0, r:"A spin exists because of two factors — stall plus yaw — and recovery must correct both: reduce AOA to break the stall and use opposite rudder against the yaw. Holding aft stick keeps the wings stalled regardless of rudder position.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.10"},
    {q:"Wingtip vortices form because air flows:", o:["From the wing root toward the fuselage","From the low-pressure region above the wing down around the tip","Chordwise off the trailing edge at high speed","From the high-pressure region below the wingtip up toward the low-pressure region above it"], a:3, r:"Lift means higher pressure below than above; at the tip that air escapes upward around the edge and combines with the downwash into a fast-spinning trailing vortex. The flow direction is low-to-high in reverse — it always leaks from high pressure toward low.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.36"},
    {q:"Which statement about L/DMAX is FALSE?", o:["It reflects the efficiency of the wing, not the engine","It is located at the bottom of the total drag curve","It is the AOA that produces the maximum amount of lift","Any movement away from it increases total drag"], a:2, r:"L/DMAX AOA maximizes the ratio of lift to drag — not the amount of lift, which peaks at CLmax AOA. The other statements are all true: minimum total drag, drag rising on either side of it, and wing (not engine) efficiency.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 2.41"},
    {q:"Although stall SPEED varies with weight, altitude, and configuration, the stalling ANGLE OF ATTACK for a given airfoil:", o:["Decreases as altitude increases","Increases with gross weight","Remains constant","Varies directly with true stall speed"], a:2, r:"CLmax AOA is a property of the airfoil and stays the same; what changes with weight, altitude, power, and configuration is the speed at which that AOA is reached. Tying stalling AOA to weight, altitude, or stall speed confuses the fixed aerodynamic limit with the variable airspeed.", ref:"Ch 2, EO 3.6"},
    {q:"Landing distance will increase when:", o:["Weight increases","Humidity decreases","Temperature decreases","Altitude decreases"], a:0, r:"A heavier airplane needs a greater airspeed to support it, which greatly lengthens the landing rollout. Decreasing altitude, temperature, or humidity all increase air density, lowering the required landing velocity and shortening the distance.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"Stabilized in level flight in the region of reverse command, to fly faster and remain level you must ___ AOA and ___ power.", o:["Increase; increase","Decrease; decrease","Increase; decrease","Decrease; increase"], a:1, r:"In reverse command, velocity and throttle are inversely related: once stabilized at the faster airspeed, the lower AOA means less induced drag, so less power is required than at the slower speed. Adding power to accelerate and keeping it there overshoots; raising AOA would slow the airplane further.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.50"},
    {q:"Which of the following is NOT one of the three major factors that decrease air density for takeoff performance?", o:["An increasing headwind","Increasing air temperature","Increasing airfield elevation","Increasing humidity"], a:0, r:"The three density-reducing factors are elevation, temperature, and humidity. A headwind affects ground speed and takeoff distance, but it does not change the density of the air.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"Climbing to a higher cruise altitude, the pilot physically advances the throttle, yet total fuel flow decreases because:", o:["Air density increases with altitude","Colder temperatures at altitude make turbine engines more fuel efficient","Ground speed decreases with altitude","The engine develops more thrust in thinner air"], a:1, r:"The throttle setting rises to compensate for lower density, but the rapidly falling temperature makes the turbine engine more efficient — requiring less fuel for a given power. Density decreases (not increases) with altitude, thrust does not grow in thin air, and TAS actually increases.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.47"},
    {q:"Your engine fails and a suitable field lies at the outer edge of your gliding distance. You should fly:", o:["CLmax AOA, to minimize your sink rate","Just below redline airspeed, to cover ground quickly","The minimum power deficit velocity (maximum glide endurance)","L/DMAX AOA, for minimum angle of descent (maximum glide range)"], a:3, r:"Reaching the most distant point requires maximum glide range — minimum descent angle at L/DMAX AOA, where drag is least. Maximum glide endurance (flown slower than L/DMAX) buys time, not distance; CLmax AOA is the stall; and high speed dumps altitude into parasite drag.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.48"},
    {q:"On the power curve, any straight line drawn from the origin represents:", o:["A constant altitude","A constant ratio of fuel flow to velocity","The accelerated stall boundary","A constant angle of attack"], a:1, r:"Because power (fuel flow) is plotted against velocity, a line through the origin holds their ratio constant; the tangent line gives the minimum ratio that keeps the airplane airborne — maximum range for props. AOA, altitude, and stall boundaries are not what the origin lines depict.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.46"},
    {q:"The 20 percent takeoff safety margin above power-off stall speed exists to:", o:["Compensate for the loss of ground effect","Minimize operation in the region of reverse command and allow shallow turns after takeoff","Prevent exceeding the limit load factor on rotation","Guarantee clearance over 50-foot obstacles"], a:1, r:"The margin keeps the airplane clear of the region of reverse command and permits shallow turns after takeoff — especially important during an engine failure. It is not a structural, obstacle, or ground effect provision.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.42"},
    {q:"When transitioning from aerodynamic braking to mechanical braking during the landing rollout, a common procedure is to:", o:["Hold the nose off the runway until reaching taxi speed","Lower the flaps fully to increase drag","Apply maximum wheel braking while lift is still high","Raise the flaps to decrease lift and transfer the airplane's weight to the wheels"], a:3, r:"Mechanical braking is only effective once enough weight rests on the wheels, so raising the flaps kills lift and loads the tires. Keeping the nose up prolongs aerodynamic braking rather than transitioning, and braking hard while the wing still lifts is ineffective.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"During a crosswind takeoff at low airspeed, the nosewheel provides additional directional stability because:", o:["The nosewheel energizes airflow over the rudder","The ailerons cannot be deflected on the ground","The rudder loses effectiveness at low airspeeds","Lateral stability steers the airplane down the runway"], a:2, r:"Until the rudder becomes effective at higher airspeed, nosewheel contact with the runway supplements directional control. Ailerons work on the ground (they are held into the wind), and lateral stability is what tries to roll the airplane — it does not steer it.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.43"},
    {q:"A 50-pound weight is placed at a station 100 inches from the datum. The moment it creates is:", o:["5,000 lb-in","2 lb-in","150 lb-in","500 lb-in"], a:0, r:"Moment equals weight times arm: 50 lb x 100 in = 5,000 lb-in. Balancing the board requires an equal 5,000 lb-in moment on the other side — any combination of weight and distance whose product matches. Adding or dividing the numbers gives the distractor values.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.3"},
    {q:"ATC directs you to expedite your climb to an assigned altitude. You should fly:", o:["L/DMAX airspeed, for minimum total drag","Maximum rate of climb, gaining the most altitude in the least time","Maximum endurance airspeed, to minimize fuel flow","Maximum angle of climb, gaining the most altitude in the least distance"], a:1, r:"Expediting a climb is a time problem — maximum rate of climb gives the greatest vertical velocity. Maximum angle of climb solves an obstacle-distance problem instead, and endurance or L/DMAX profiles are cruise and glide references, not expedited climb profiles.", ref:"Ch 3, EO 2.44"},
    {q:"In a right turn the ball is displaced to the right — the inside of the turn. The airplane is in a ___ and the pilot should apply ___.", o:["Slip; left rudder","Slip; right rudder","Skid; left rudder","Skid; right rudder"], a:1, r:"Ball displaced in the same direction as the turn indicates a slip (insufficient rudder), with gravity pulling the ball to the inside. The correction is to step on the ball — right rudder here. A skid would throw the ball opposite the turn, to the outside.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.51"},
    {q:"Why is a stall from a skidding turn more dangerous than a stall from a slipping turn?", o:["A skid increases turn radius, delaying the recovery","A slip stall cannot be recovered at any altitude","A skid stall occurs at a much lower angle of attack","A skidded turn stall rolls the airplane inverted, while a slip stall rolls it toward wings level"], a:3, r:"Stalling from a skid rolls the airplane inverted — probably fatal at low altitude — while stalling from a slip produces the safer roll toward wings level. Stalling AOA is the same in both cases, and a skid actually decreases turn radius.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.52"},
    {q:"On takeoff at high power and low airspeed with the relative wind below the thrust line, a propeller airplane will tend to:", o:["Yaw right from P-factor, requiring left rudder","Show no P-factor, since power is high","Pitch up from slipstream swirl","Yaw left from P-factor, requiring right rudder"], a:3, r:"With the relative wind displaced below the thrust line, the down-going blade on the right side meets the relative wind at a greater angle and makes more thrust, yawing the nose left — countered with right rudder. High power is a requirement for P-factor, not a preventer, and slipstream swirl produces yaw, not pitch.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.53"},
    {q:"In a level turn, if the pilot does not increase the total lift vector, the airplane will:", o:["Hold altitude as long as airspeed is constant","Roll out of the turn on its own","Descend, because the vertical lift component becomes less than weight","Climb, because the load factor increases"], a:2, r:"Banking splits lift into horizontal and vertical components; only the vertical part opposes weight. Without more total lift (more AOA), weight exceeds the vertical component and the airplane loses altitude — constant airspeed does not prevent it.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.54"},
    {q:"The limit load factor is intentionally designed to be less than the elastic limit of individual components so that:", o:["The airplane can operate at its limit load with no permanent deformation, ensuring its expected service life","Pilots may exceed the limit load by 50 percent safely","The wings will depart before internal components are damaged","The airframe fails before the engine mounts do"], a:0, r:"Keeping the limit load below every component's elastic limit virtually guarantees the airplane reaches its expected service life, since normal operations then cause no permanent deformation. The 50 percent margin above limit load is the ultimate load — where structural failure is imminent, not a safe allowance.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.55"},
    {q:"Which statement about exceeding load limits is TRUE?", o:["The negative limit load factor equals the positive limit load factor","Overstress damage is always visible on the airframe","Exceeding the ultimate load factor makes structural failure imminent","Exceeding the limit load factor causes the wings to depart the airplane"], a:2, r:"Beyond the ultimate load factor, something major will break. The wing will not depart merely from exceeding the limit load, overstress can occur without visible damage (which is why it must be reported), and negative limits are smaller than positive ones because pilots cannot sustain much negative acceleration.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.55"},
    {q:"A single V-n diagram is valid only for a particular:", o:["Runway and wind condition","Pilot and crew complement","Engine power setting","Weight, altitude, and configuration"], a:3, r:"The V-n diagram plots load factor against indicated airspeed and represents the maneuvering envelope for one weight, altitude, and configuration — change any of them and the envelope shifts. Crew, power, and runway conditions are not the defining parameters.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.56"},
    {q:"Below maneuver speed, an airplane cannot be overstressed by pilot input because:", o:["The accelerated stall lines lie above the ultimate load factor","Dynamic pressure is too low to move the control surfaces","It will stall before the limit load factor can be reached","The controls are too heavy to deflect fully"], a:2, r:"Below Va the accelerated stall line is reached before the limit load line — the wing runs out of lift (stalls) before it can generate a damaging load factor. Control heaviness and surface effectiveness are not the mechanism, and the stall lines sit below, not above, the structural limits at these speeds.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.56"},
    {q:"Which statement about wake turbulence near the runway is TRUE?", o:["Vortices stream from the wings throughout the entire takeoff roll","There is no vortex hazard on the runway surface before the preceding departing aircraft's liftoff point","Vortices vanish the instant the generating aircraft touches down","Only heavy aircraft generate a vortex hazard"], a:1, r:"Vortices are a by-product of lift, generated from rotation (nosewheel liftoff) until the nosewheel touches down on landing — so the runway before the liftoff point is clean. All lift-producing aircraft, regardless of size, create vortices, and on landing the hazard ends at nosewheel touchdown, not main-gear touchdown.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.58"},
    {q:"On final approach, wind shear suddenly reduces your headwind component by 20 knots. Initially, your:", o:["Indicated airspeed decreases about 20 knots, decreasing lift","Performance is unaffected because the airplane rides with the air mass","Ground speed falls while lift stays constant","Indicated airspeed increases about 20 knots, increasing lift"], a:0, r:"Losing 20 knots of headwind instantly reduces the relative wind, so indicated airspeed — and with it dynamic pressure and lift — drops until the airplane stabilizes in the new air mass. The gain scenario belongs to an increasing headwind, and the airplane only behaves 'as if nothing happened' after it has restabilized, which takes time you may not have on short final.", ref:"Ch 4, EO 2.60"}
  ]
}
];
